  "id": "gr-nai",
  "connector": "〜ない",
  "gloss": "casual verbs: do and don’t",
  "title": "〜る・〜ない — casual “do” and “don’t”",
  "pattern": "dictionary form ({行|い}く) / ない form ({行|い}かない)",
  "explanation": "With friends, family and a partner, verbs drop ます. “Do” is the dictionary form, the one on every word card: {行|い}く, {食|た}べる. “Don’t” is the ない form: {行|い}かない, {食|た}べない. For る-verbs like {食|た}べる, swap る for ない. For the others, the last sound moves to the あ row: {行|い}く → {行|い}かない, {飲|の}む → {飲|の}まない. する → しない, {来|く}る → {来|こ}ない, and ある → ない.",
  "natural": "This is how you’ll talk with a partner and friends: {今日|きょう}、{行|い}く？ — ううん、{行|い}かない。",
  "stiff": "Plain forms sound rude to staff, strangers and a partner’s parents: keep ます there. And “there isn’t” is just ない, never あらない.",
